// Test: bugfixes/plugin_discovery_target_block_alias_hint
// Grammar: framework_block (unrecognized top-level block)
// Semantic: unknown-block hard error (SYN001) with alias-name hint
// Tracking: 590e61575e58
// Fixed in: compiler 0.33.84
//
// Regression pin for dashboard fingerprint 590e61575e58
// (PLUGIN-DISCOVERY-TARGET-BLOCK).
//
// Pre-0.33.80: a top-level `target:\n\tframe.server` block silently
// passed through the plugin expander and the HIR builder's catch-all
// arm, producing a WASM with zero plugin block expansion (no
// _http_route, no _http_listen, no user endpoints) — apps compiled
// successfully but the server never registered any routes.
//
// 0.33.80: COMPILER-IMPLICIT-PLUGIN-ACTIVATION landed. All unknown
// FrameworkBlocks became SYN001 hard errors, so the silent-drop
// symptom disappeared. But the generic error text ("declare the
// owning plugin") pointed users the wrong direction — `target:`
// isn't owned by any plugin; the user meant to write `plugins:`.
//
// 0.33.84: added targeted hint for common `plugins:` aliases
// (target, targets, plugin, uses, requires) so the error message
// says "'target:' is not a Clean Language block header. Replace
// `target:` with `plugins:`" instead of the generic
// UnknownBlockType text.
//
// This pin is a POSITIVE control: the corrected form (`plugins:`)
// compiles cleanly. The regression harness matches on stdout, so
// baking the negative case (SYN001 on `target:`) requires a
// different harness — for the negative side the Rust unit test
// plugins::expander::tests::test_unknown_block_is_hard_error (from
// 0.33.80) covers the hard-error invariant; the alias-hint text
// is covered by the ai-generated repro in this file's header
// comment and the direct compile output.
//
// Fixed in: compiler 0.33.84
// Expected output:
//   ok

start:
	printl("ok")
